## TL;DR  
The “`text-anchor` missing + `symbol-placement === 'point'`” test is **not a reliable shield detector**.  
A lot of ordinary point-symbol layers (place-name, POI, city-label, etc.) have exactly those two properties, so they get mis-tagged, while many real shield layers do **not** meet the test (they often set `text-anchor: 'center'` explicitly, or they use a zoom-function for `symbol-placement`).  

A more robust heuristic is to look for the **combination of icon-image + text-field that references a route reference**, plus a few “shield-specific” layout flags that are almost always present in the OSM-based styles (OSM-Bright, OSM-Liberty, OpenMapTiles, etc.).  

Below is a concise checklist, a ready-to-paste JavaScript function that implements it, and an explanation of why each cue matters.

---

## 1. Why the current heuristic fails

| Property | Typical value for many point-symbol layers | Typical value for shield layers |
|----------|--------------------------------------------|---------------------------------|
| `text-anchor` | **undefined** → defaults to `"center"` (most POI / place-name layers never set it) | Often **explicitly set** to `"center"` (or left undefined) – the same as above |
| `symbol-placement` | `"point"` for any point-symbol (places, POIs, icons) | `"point"` **or** a zoom-function (`{stops: …}`) that switches to `"line"` at higher zooms – the simple equality test will miss the latter |

Result: **high false-positive rate** (plain place-name layers) and **high false-negative rate** (real shields that use a function or set `text-anchor` explicitly).

---

## 2. What *actually* distinguishes a route-shield layer

| Cue | What it looks like in the style | Why it’s a good shield indicator |
|-----|--------------------------------|----------------------------------|
| **`layout['icon-image']`** contains a **placeholder** (`{network}`, `{ref_length}`, `{ref}`) **or** matches the pattern `*_{ref_length}` (e.g. `us-interstate_2`, `road_3`) | `"icon-image": "{network}_{ref_length}"`  <br>or `"icon-image": ["concat", ["get", "network"], "_", ["to-string", ["get", "ref_length"]]]` | Shields are the only symbols that build the icon name from feature properties. |
| **`layout['text-field']`** contains `{ref}` (or an expression that resolves to the route number) | `"text-field": "{ref}"`  <br>or `["get", "ref"]` | The numeric part of a shield is always rendered as a text label. |
| **`layout['icon-text-fit']`** is set to `"both"` or `"width"` (optional but common) | `"icon-text-fit": "both"` | Only stretchable shield badges use this; plain POI icons never do. |
| **`layout['icon-allow-overlap']`** (or `icon-overlap: "always"`) is **true** | `"icon-allow-overlap": true` | Shields must be drawn even when they overlap other symbols; most place-name layers keep the default `false`. |
| **`layout['text-allow-overlap']`** is **true** (or `text-overlap: "always"`) | `"text-allow-overlap": true` | The route number must stay visible even when it collides with other text. |
| **`layout['icon-size']`** is often a **function of `ref_length`** (e.g. larger badges for longer numbers) | `"icon-size": ["interpolate", ["linear"], ["get", "ref_length"], 1, 0.8, 6, 1.2]` | POI icons rarely vary size by a property called `ref_length`. |
| **`paint['icon-color']`** is **absent** or **`'black'`** (shields are usually SDF) **and** the sprite entry has `sdf: true` | No `icon-color` or `icon-color: 'black'` + sprite entry `sdf:true` | Only SDF shields react to `icon-color`; ordinary raster icons ignore it. |
| **Source-layer name** often contains *transportation* or *road* (e.g. `transportation_name`, `road_shield`, `shield`) | `"source-layer": "transportation_name"` | Not a hard rule, but a useful sanity check when you have the source schema. |
| **`layout['symbol-placement']`** is either `"point"` **or** a **zoom-function** that switches to `"line"` at higher zooms | `{ "base": 1, "stops": [[7, "point"], [8, "line"]] }` | Shields appear as points at low zoom and as line-anchored symbols on the road at higher zoom. |
| **`layout['text-font']`** references a **glyph set that ships with the style** (e.g. `["Open Sans Regular", "Arial Unicode MS Regular"]`) – this alone isn’t unique, but combined with the other cues it helps rule out layers that never show text. | | |

If **any** of the first two cues (`icon-image` placeholder + `text-field` `{ref}`) are missing, you can safely assume the layer is **not** a shield. The remaining cues are optional refinements that filter out false positives (e.g., a point-symbol that also uses a placeholder for a different purpose).

---

## 3. A practical detection function

```js
/**
 * Returns true if a symbol layer looks like a route-shield layer.
 *
 * @param {object} layer – a layer object from a MapLibre style (already parsed)
 * @returns {boolean}
 */
function isRouteShieldLayer(layer) {
  // 1️⃣ Must be a symbol layer
  if (layer.type !== 'symbol') return false;

  const layout = layer.layout || {};

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣ Core shield signature: icon-image built from feature properties
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  const iconImage = layout['icon-image'];
  const hasIconPlaceholder =
    typeof iconImage === 'string' && /\{(network|ref_length|ref)\}/.test(iconImage) ||
    Array.isArray(iconImage) && iconImage.some(v => typeof v === 'string' && /\{(network|ref_length|ref)\}/.test(v));

  // 3️⃣ Core shield signature: text-field shows the route number
  const textField = layout['text-field'];
  const hasRefPlaceholder =
    typeof textField === 'string' && /\{ref\}/.test(textField) ||
    Array.isArray(textField) && textField.some(v => typeof v === 'string' && /\{ref\}/.test(v));

  if (!hasIconPlaceholder || !hasRefPlaceholder) return false;

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣ Optional but strong clues – if present, they reinforce the guess
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  const optionalClues = [];

  // icon-text-fit (stretchable badge)
  if (['both', 'width', 'height'].includes(layout['icon-text-fit'])) optionalClues.push('icon-text-fit');

  // overlap flags – shields usually allow overlap
  if (layout['icon-allow-overlap'] === true || layout['icon-overlap'] === 'always')
    optionalClues.push('icon-allow-overlap');

  if (layout['text-allow-overlap'] === true || layout['text-overlap'] === 'always')
    optionalClues.push('text-allow-overlap');

  // size function that uses ref_length
  const iconSize = layout['icon-size'];
  if (Array.isArray(iconSize) && iconSize.some(v => v === 'ref_length')) optionalClues.push('icon-size-ref_length');

  // symbol-placement can be a function; we accept both plain and functional forms
  const symPlace = layout['symbol-placement'];
  const isPointPlacement =
    symPlace === 'point' ||
    (symPlace && typeof symPlace === 'object' && ('stops' in symPlace || 'base' in symPlace));
  if (!isPointPlacement) optionalClues.push('non-point-placement');

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 5️⃣ Decision: core signature + at least one reinforcing clue
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // If we have the core signature but none of the optional clues, we still treat it as a shield
  // because the core signature is already very specific.  The optional clues are only used
  // to avoid false-positives when the core signature is accidentally matched by a custom
  // icon that also uses placeholders.
  return true;
}

/* -----------------------------------------------------------------
   Example usage on a style object
   ----------------------------------------------------------------- */
function auditStyle(style) {
  const shields = [];
  const others = [];

  for (const layer of style.layers) {
    if (isRouteShieldLayer(layer)) shields.push(layer.id);
    else others.push(layer.id);
  }

  console.log('Detected shield layers:', shields);
  console.log('Other layers flagged as “not shield” :', others);
}
```

### How the function works

| Step | What we check | Why it matters |
|------|---------------|----------------|
| **1** | `layer.type === 'symbol'` | Shields are always symbol layers. |
| **2** | `icon-image` contains `{network}` or `{ref_length}` (or any placeholder) | Only shields build the badge name from feature properties. |
| **3** | `text-field` contains `{ref}` | The numeric part of a shield is always rendered as text. |
| **4** | Optional clues (`icon-text-fit`, overlap flags, size function, placement function) | Reinforces the guess and filters out edge-cases where a custom point-symbol also uses placeholders for a different purpose. |
| **5** | Return `true` if the core signature (2 + 3) is present. | The core signature alone is already highly specific; the optional clues are just safety nets. |

You can tighten the rule further by requiring **at least one** optional clue, but that will start missing shields that happen to omit those flags (e.g., a minimal custom style that only sets `icon-image` and `text-field`). The balance you choose depends on how aggressive you want the detector to be.

