## 2. What *actually* distinguishes a route-shield layer

| Cue | What it looks like in the style | Why it’s a good shield indicator |
|-----|--------------------------------|----------------------------------|
| **`layout['icon-image']`** contains a **placeholder** (`{network}`, `{ref_length}`, `{ref}`) **or** matches the pattern `*_{ref_length}` (e.g. `us-interstate_2`, `road_3`) | `"icon-image": "{network}_{ref_length}"`  <br>or `"icon-image": ["concat", ["get", "network"], "_", ["to-string", ["get", "ref_length"]]]` | Shields are the only symbols that build the icon name from feature properties. |
| **`layout['text-field']`** contains `{ref}` (or an expression that resolves to the route number) | `"text-field": "{ref}"`  <br>or `["get", "ref"]` | The numeric part of a shield is always taken from the `ref` property. |
| **`layout['icon-text-fit']`** is set to `"both"` or `"width"` (optional but common) | `"icon-text-fit": "both"` | Only stretchable shields use `icon-text-fit`; most other point symbols leave it at the default `"none"`. |
| **`layout['icon-allow-overlap']`** (or `icon-ignore-placement`) is **`true`** | `"icon-allow-overlap": true` | Shields must be drawn even when they overlap other symbols; most place-name layers keep the default `false`. |
| **`layout['text-allow-overlap']`** is **`true`** (or `text-ignore-placement`) | `"text-allow-overlap": true` | The route number must stay visible even when it collides with other text. |
| **`layout['symbol-placement']`** is either `"point"` **or** a **zoom-function** that switches to `"line"` at higher zooms (i.e. an object with a `stops` array) | `{ "symbol-placement": { "base": 1, "stops": [[7, "point"], [8, "line"]] } }` | Shields appear as points at low zoom and as line-labels at higher zoom. |
| **`source-layer`** (or `source` name) is a transportation-related layer (`transportation_name`, `road_shield`, `highway_shield`, etc.) | `"source-layer": "transportation_name"` | Not a hard rule (custom sources exist) but a useful sanity check. |
| **`paint['text-color']`** is often set (e.g. `#000` or `#fff`) while most POI layers rely on the default black. | `"text-color": "#fff"` | Optional, but shields usually have a contrasting text colour. |

> **Bottom line:** The *presence of a dynamic `icon-image` that depends on feature properties* together with a *dynamic `text-field` that pulls the route reference* is the **signature** of a shield. All the other flags are “nice-to-have” refinements that reduce false positives.

---

## 3. A practical detection function

```js
/**
 * Returns true if a symbol layer looks like a route-shield layer.
 *
 * @param {object} layer – a layer object from a MapLibre style (already verified to be type: 'symbol')
 * @returns {boolean}
 */
function isShieldLayer(layer) {
  // 1️⃣ Must be a symbol layer
  if (layer.type !== 'symbol') return false;

  const layout = layer.layout || {};

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣ Icon-image must be *dynamic* (contains a placeholder or a concat expression)
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  const iconImg = layout['icon-image'];
  const iconIsDynamic =
    typeof iconImg === 'string' && /\{(network|ref_length|ref)\}/.test(iconImg) ||
    Array.isArray(iconImg) && (
      // simple concat pattern: ["concat", ["get","network"], "_", ["to-string", ["get","ref_length"]]]
      iconImg[0] === 'concat' && iconImg.some(v => Array.isArray(v) && v[0] === 'get')
    );

  if (!iconIsDynamic) return false;

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣ Text-field must reference the route reference (usually {ref})
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  const textField = layout['text-field'];
  const textIsDynamic =
    typeof textField === 'string' && /\{ref\}/.test(textField) ||
    Array.isArray(textField) && (
      // ["get","ref"] or ["to-string",["get","ref"]]
      textField.some(v => Array.isArray(v) && v[0] === 'get' && v[1] === 'ref')
    );

  if (!textIsDynamic) return false;

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣ Optional but strong clues – if any are present, they reinforce the guess
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  const hasFit = layout['icon-text-fit'] && layout['icon-text-fit'] !== 'none';
  const allowOverlap = layout['icon-allow-overlap'] === true ||
                       layout['icon-ignore-placement'] === true;
  const textAllowOverlap = layout['text-allow-overlap'] === true ||
                           layout['text-ignore-placement'] === true;

  // If the layer explicitly disables overlap, it is *unlikely* to be a shield.
  if (layout['icon-allow-overlap'] === false && layout['icon-ignore-placement'] !== true) {
    return false;
  }

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 5️⃣ Symbol-placement: accept "point" or a zoom-function that yields "point"
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  const placement = layout['symbol-placement'];
  const placementIsPoint =
    placement === 'point' ||
    (typeof placement === 'object' && placement.stops && placement.stops.some(s => s[1] === 'point'));

  if (!placementIsPoint) return false;

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 6️⃣ (Optional) source-layer name hint – only keep if you want stricter filtering
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  const sourceLayer = layer['source-layer'] || '';
  const transportHint = /transport|road|highway|shield/.test(sourceLayer);

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // Final decision – we require the two mandatory dynamic clues,
  // and at least one of the optional “shield-ish” clues.
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  const optionalClues = [hasFit, allowOverlap, textAllowOverlap, transportHint].filter(Boolean).length;
  return optionalClues > 0;   // true = shield
}
```

### How the function works

| Step | What it checks | Why it matters |
|------|----------------|----------------|
| 1️⃣ | `type === 'symbol'` | Shields are always symbol layers. |
| 2️⃣ | `icon-image` contains `{network}` / `{ref_length}` or a concat expression that pulls those properties | This is the *defining* pattern of shield icons. |
| 3️⃣ | `text-field` contains `{ref}` (or a `["get","ref"]` expression) | The numeric part of a shield always comes from the `ref` attribute. |
| 4️⃣ | `icon-text-fit`, `icon-allow-overlap`, `text-allow-overlap` | These flags are *almost always* set for shields; they help weed out POI layers that also have dynamic icons (e.g., category-based icons). |
| 5️⃣ | `symbol-placement` is `"point"` **or** a zoom-function that yields `"point"` | Shields start as point symbols at low zoom. |
| 6️⃣ | `source-layer` name contains a transport-related keyword (optional) | Adds an extra safety net for custom styles. |

If you only need a **quick filter**, you can stop after steps 2 + 3. If you want **high precision**, keep the optional clues (step 4-6) as shown.

---

## 4. How to integrate this into your audit script

```js
// Assuming `style` is the parsed JSON style object
const shieldLayers = style.layers.filter(l => isShieldLayer(l));

console.log(`Found ${shieldLayers.length} shield layer(s):`);
shieldLayers.forEach(l => console.log(`- ${l.id}`));
```

You can also tag the layer with a custom property for later processing:

```js
style.layers.forEach(l => {
  if (isShieldLayer(l)) l.metadata = { ...(l.metadata || {}), isShield: true };
});
```

